What happens when a design system escapes the comfy confines of Figma and lands in production? Lots of duct tape, delight, and a few sharp corners.

## The Gap Between Design and Code

<button>hello</button>

Every design system starts with beautiful intentions. Pixel-perfect components in Figma. Comprehensive documentation. Clear naming conventions. And then reality hits.

Browsers have opinions. Accessibility requirements add complexity. Edge cases multiply like rabbits. The pristine vision meets the messy world.

## What Actually Ships

The design systems that work aren't the most comprehensive—they're the most pragmatic. They acknowledge that:

- **Tokens are contracts**, not suggestions
- **Components need escape hatches** for edge cases
- **Documentation is never done**, it's maintained
- **Adoption beats perfection**

## The Duct Tape Moments

Every mature system has them. The workaround for that one legacy page. The component variant that exists for a single use case. The prop that nobody remembers why it exists.

> A design system without duct tape is a design system that hasn't shipped.

## Building for Change

The best design systems I've worked with embrace evolution. They version aggressively, deprecate gracefully, and measure adoption relentlessly.

They know that the goal isn't a perfect component library—it's enabling teams to build consistent experiences faster than they could alone.

## The Human Layer

Ultimately, design systems are about people. The best technical implementation fails if designers and developers don't adopt it. Invest in the relationships as much as the code.

